_2026_w12_part2_spark","before":[{"title":"G6 T3 W12 Part 2 - Build and test a night-light","spark_text":"Pressing button A gives the program an input. The number shown on the screen is an output.","why_it_matters":"Build the night-light response and check its boundary.","question":"Name the input and output in the example you just read."},{"title":"G6 T3 W12 - Complete the improvement - Words in use 2","spark_text":"We test the program by pressing the button three times. The feedback says to check the displayed total after a reset.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"A press changes the state · Part 2","spark_text":"A device starts with its light off. Each new button press changes the light to the opposite state: off becomes on, and on becomes off. This is a toggle. After two presses, the light is off again. The program must remember its current state between presses.","why_it_matters":"Remembering state lets the same input have the correct next effect.","question":"Starting with the light off, what state should it have after three presses? Explain."}},{"id":"grade7_t3_2026_w01_part1_spark","before":[{"title":"G7 T3 W01 Part 1 - Find a spreadsheet cell","spark_text":"Row 3 holds all the information about the chess club. Column B holds the number of students in each club.","why_it_matters":"Find a cell using its column letter and row number.","question":"B3 uses column B and row 3. Which part of C4 names the row?"}],"after":{"title":"Find the right spreadsheet cell · Part 1","spark_text":"A spreadsheet has columns labelled A, B and C, and rows numbered 1, 2 and 3. A column runs down the sheet; a row runs across it. A cell address combines the column letter and row number. B3 means column B, row 3.","why_it_matters":"Cell addresses let you point to an exact place in a spreadsheet.","question":"In C2, which part names the column and which part names the row?"}},{"id":"grade7_t3_2026_w01_part2_spark","before":[{"title":"G7 T3 W01 Part 2 - Practise cells and formulas","spark_text":"Row 3 holds all the information about the chess club. Column B holds the number of students in each club.","why_it_matters":"Find and explain table values, then use three spreadsheet formulas.","question":"B3 uses column B and row 3. Which part of C4 names the row?"}],"after":{"title":"Find the right spreadsheet cell · Part 2","spark_text":"A spreadsheet has columns labelled A, B and C, and rows numbered 1, 2 and 3. A column runs down the sheet; a row runs across it. A cell address combines the column letter and row number. B3 means column B, row 3.","why_it_matters":"Cell addresses let you point to an exact place in a spreadsheet.","question":"In C2, which part names the column and which part names the row?"}},{"id":"grade7_t3_2026_w02_part1_spark","before":[{"title":"G7 T3 W02 Part 1 - Read spreadsheet cells","spark_text":"Row 3 holds all the information about the chess club. Column B holds the number of students in each club.","why_it_matters":"Find cell addresses and explain two values in a table.","question":"B3 uses column B and row 3. Which part of C4 names the row?"},{"title":"G7 T3 W02 - Spreadsheet cells, charts and formulas - Words in use 1","spark_text":"Cell B3 contains the number of students in the club. The table puts each club name beside its student count. The formula =B2+B3 adds two cell values.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"Find the right spreadsheet cell · Part 1","spark_text":"A spreadsheet has columns labelled A, B and C, and rows numbered 1, 2 and 3. A column runs down the sheet; a row runs across it. A cell address combines the column letter and row number. B3 means column B, row 3.","why_it_matters":"Cell addresses let you point to an exact place in a spreadsheet.","question":"In C2, which part names the column and which part names the row?"}},{"id":"grade7_t3_2026_w02_part2_spark","before":[{"title":"G7 T3 W02 Part 2 - Build a column chart","spark_text":"The table puts club names beside their student counts. The chart uses bars to compare the club counts.","why_it_matters":"Make a labelled column chart and explain one value.","question":"What is easier to compare in a bar chart than in a table?"},{"title":"G7 T3 W02 - Spreadsheet cells, charts and formulas - Words in use 2","spark_text":"The function SUM adds a range of spreadsheet values. The chart uses bars to compare the club counts.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"Find the right spreadsheet cell · Part 2","spark_text":"A spreadsheet has columns labelled A, B and C, and rows numbered 1, 2 and 3. A column runs down the sheet; a row runs across it. A cell address combines the column letter and row number. B3 means column B, row 3.","why_it_matters":"Cell addresses let you point to an exact place in a spreadsheet.","question":"In C2, which part names the column and which part names the row?"}},{"id":"grade7_t3_2026_w03_part1_spark","before":[{"title":"G7 T3 W03 Part 1 - Explain your chart","spark_text":"The formula =B2+B3 adds two cell values. The chart uses bars to compare the club counts.","why_it_matters":"Use exact values to write two conclusions about your chart.","question":"Why should you check the formula answers before making the chart?"},{"title":"G7 T3 W03 - Interpret charts and calculations - Words in use 1","spark_text":"The formula =B2+B3 adds two cell values. The total is the number of bottles collected by all teams. The average for 10, 20 and 30 is 20.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"Find the right spreadsheet cell · Part 1","spark_text":"A spreadsheet has columns labelled A, B and C, and rows numbered 1, 2 and 3. A column runs down the sheet; a row runs across it. A cell address combines the column letter and row number. B3 means column B, row 3.","why_it_matters":"Cell addresses let you point to an exact place in a spreadsheet.","question":"In C2, which part names the column and which part names the row?"}},{"id":"grade7_t3_2026_w03_part2_spark","before":[{"title":"G7 T3 W03 Part 2 - Explain spreadsheet data","spark_text":"The formula =B2+B3 adds two cell values. The chart uses bars to compare the club counts.","why_it_matters":"Use formulas and a chart to explain eight book counts.","question":"Why should you check the formula answers before making the chart?"},{"title":"G7 T3 W03 - Interpret charts and calculations - Words in use 2","spark_text":"The chart uses bars to compare the club counts. Our comparison shows that chess has more students than art.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"Read the bars · Part 2","spark_text":"A club survey counts 12 students in chess, 8 in art and 5 in music. A bar chart shows one bar for each club, using the same scale for every bar. The chess bar is tallest because 12 is the largest count. Labels tell the reader which club each bar represents.","why_it_matters":"A labelled chart makes comparisons easier to see.","question":"How many more students chose chess than music, and how would the bars show that difference?"}},{"id":"grade7_t3_2026_w04_part1_spark","before":[{"title":"G7 T3 W04 Part 1 - Plan your STEAM work","spark_text":"The paper bridge is a prototype we can test before building a stronger bridge. The small cardboard model shows the shape of the bridge.","why_it_matters":"Choose one project task and state how you will check it.","question":"What could you test with a paper prototype before building a stronger version?"},{"title":"G7 T3 W04 - Scratch delivery practice - Words in use 1","spark_text":"The deliver function moves the sprite and adds one delivery. The loop repeats the delivery three times. The variable count stores the number of button presses.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"What starts the action? · Part 1","spark_text":"An app has a button labelled Play. Pressing that button is an event. The app responds by playing a sound. The button itself is not the sound; it is the control that triggers the action. Without the event, this sound does not start.","why_it_matters":"An event connects something the user does with a response from the program.","question":"What is the event in this example, and what action does it trigger?"}},{"id":"grade7_t3_2026_w04_part2_spark","before":[{"title":"G7 T3 W04 Part 2 - Build your STEAM project","spark_text":"The paper bridge is a prototype we can test before building a stronger bridge. The small cardboard model shows the shape of the bridge.","why_it_matters":"Complete your planned task and record its result.","question":"What could you test with a paper prototype before building a stronger version?"},{"title":"G7 T3 W04 - Scratch delivery practice - Words in use 2","spark_text":"The sprite starts at position x equals zero. Click the green flag to reset the sprite and delivery count.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"What starts the action? · Part 2","spark_text":"An app has a button labelled Play. Pressing that button is an event. The app responds by playing a sound. The button itself is not the sound; it is the control that triggers the action. Without the event, this sound does not start.","why_it_matters":"An event connects something the user does with a response from the program.","question":"What is the event in this example, and what action does it trigger?"}},{"id":"grade7_t3_2026_w07_part1_spark","before":[{"title":"G7 T3 W07 Part 1 - Repeat a Scratch task","spark_text":"The function deliver groups the Scratch steps for one delivery. The loop repeats the move instruction four times.","why_it_matters":"Build a custom block and run it three times.","question":"Which repeats the delivery: the function name or the loop?"},{"title":"G7 T3 W07 - Distance warning rule - Words in use 1","spark_text":"A distance sensor measures how far away an object is. The threshold is 18 centimetres, so only larger distances trigger the warning. Pressing button A provides an input to the program.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"A reading triggers a response · Part 1","spark_text":"A robot uses a distance sensor to measure how far away an object is. Its rule says: if the distance is less than 10 cm, stop the motors. A reading of 6 cm meets that condition; a reading of 15 cm does not.","why_it_matters":"A sensor reading can become the input to a programmed decision.","question":"Should the robot stop at 8 cm? Explain using the rule."}},{"id":"grade7_t3_2026_w07_part2_spark","before":[{"title":"G7 T3 W07 Part 2 - Deliver with a Scratch loop","spark_text":"The function deliver groups the Scratch steps for one delivery. The loop repeats the move instruction four times.","why_it_matters":"Program and test a repeated delivery task.","question":"Which repeats the delivery: the function name or the loop?"},{"title":"G7 T3 W07 - Distance warning rule - Words in use 2","spark_text":"The displayed number is an output from the counter. The condition checks whether the distance is greater than 18.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"A reading triggers a response · Part 2","spark_text":"A robot uses a distance sensor to measure how far away an object is. Its rule says: if the distance is less than 10 cm, stop the motors. A reading of 6 cm meets that condition; a reading of 15 cm does not.","why_it_matters":"A sensor reading can become the input to a programmed decision.","question":"Should the robot stop at 8 cm? Explain using the rule."}},{"id":"grade7_t3_2026_w08_part2_spark","before":[{"title":"G7 T3 W08 Part 2 - Organise three files","spark_text":"I save my spreadsheet as a file named ClubResults.xlsx. The folder keeps my three project files together.","why_it_matters":"Name, sort and reopen three files.","question":"Why is it easier to find three project files when they share one folder?"},{"title":"G7 T3 W08 - Compare a Scratch change - Words in use 2","spark_text":"The sprite starts at position x equals zero. Click the green flag to reset the sprite and delivery count.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"What starts the action? · Part 2","spark_text":"An app has a button labelled Play. Pressing that button is an event. The app responds by playing a sound. The button itself is not the sound; it is the control that triggers the action. Without the event, this sound does not start.","why_it_matters":"An event connects something the user does with a response from the program.","question":"What is the event in this example, and what action 