k_text":"A robot uses a distance sensor to measure how far away an object is. Its rule says: if the distance is less than 10 cm, stop the motors. A reading of 6 cm meets that condition; a reading of 15 cm does not.","why_it_matters":"A sensor reading can become the input to a programmed decision.","question":"Should the robot stop at 8 cm? Explain using the rule."}},{"id":"grade7_t3_2026_w11_part2_spark","before":[{"title":"G7 T3 W11 Part 2 - Compare a project change","spark_text":"A test checks whether the project meets its stated goal. The feedback names one project result that needs to improve.","why_it_matters":"Use before-and-after tests to explain one display change.","question":"What result will you test, and what feedback would help you improve it?"},{"title":"G7 T3 W11 - Improve the warning - Words in use 2","spark_text":"The receiver shows an icon when the message is ALERT. The displayed number is an output from the counter.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"Explain what changed · Part 2","spark_text":"A student compares two versions of a poster. In the first, small text was hard to read. In the second, the text is larger, and three classmates can read it from the same distance. A reflection explains a change, its reason and the evidence of its effect.","why_it_matters":"Specific evidence makes a reflection more useful than simply saying 'It is better.'","question":"Which change did the student make, and what evidence suggests it helped?"}},{"id":"grade7_t3_2026_w12_part1_spark","before":[{"title":"G7 T3 W12 Part 1 - Practise your Mandrake demo","spark_text":"The sensor measures the light around the micro:bit. The threshold is 100, so a reading of 99 is below it.","why_it_matters":"Explain the warning system while showing its test results.","question":"The threshold is 100. Is a sensor reading of 99 below it?"},{"title":"G7 T3 W12 - Finish the feedback improvement - Words in use 1","spark_text":"A distance sensor measures how far away an object is. The threshold is 18 centimetres, so only larger distances trigger the warning. The micro:bit uses radio to send ALERT to the receiver.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"A reading triggers a response · Part 1","spark_text":"A robot uses a distance sensor to measure how far away an object is. Its rule says: if the distance is less than 10 cm, stop the motors. A reading of 6 cm meets that condition; a reading of 15 cm does not.","why_it_matters":"A sensor reading can become the input to a programmed decision.","question":"Should the robot stop at 8 cm? Explain using the rule."}},{"id":"grade7_t3_2026_w12_part2_spark","before":[{"title":"G7 T3 W12 Part 2 - Demonstrate your warning system","spark_text":"The sensor measures the light around the micro:bit. The threshold is 100, so a reading of 99 is below it.","why_it_matters":"Demonstrate and explain the Mandrake warning system.","question":"The threshold is 100. Is a sensor reading of 99 below it?"},{"title":"G7 T3 W12 - Finish the feedback improvement - Words in use 2","spark_text":"The receiver shows an icon when the message is ALERT. The displayed number is an output from the counter.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"Explain what changed · Part 2","spark_text":"A student compares two versions of a poster. In the first, small text was hard to read. In the second, the text is larger, and three classmates can read it from the same distance. A reflection explains a change, its reason and the evidence of its effect.","why_it_matters":"Specific evidence makes a reflection more useful than simply saying 'It is better.'","question":"Which change did the student make, and what evidence suggests it helped?"}},{"id":"grade8_t3_2026_w01_part1_spark","before":[{"title":"G8 T3 W01 Part 1 - Plan a lunch app","spark_text":"Choosing a lunch gives the app an input. The order message is the app output.","why_it_matters":"Describe how a lunch app uses a student’s choice to show an order.","question":"Name the input and output in the example you just read."}],"after":{"title":"A plan needs a clear first step · Part 1","spark_text":"A group wants to create a guide for new students. It names the audience, lists the three questions the guide must answer, and assigns someone to draft each answer. A project plan describes the intended result and manageable steps toward it.","why_it_matters":"A specific plan makes it easier to begin and check progress.","question":"What should the group decide about its audience and content before making the guide?"}},{"id":"grade8_t3_2026_w01_part2_spark","before":[{"title":"G8 T3 W01 Part 2 - Map your lunch app","spark_text":"A button click is an event that starts the next action. The variable meal stores the chosen lunch.","why_it_matters":"Connect three lunch-app screens using four user actions.","question":"What event could change the value stored in the meal variable?"}],"after":{"title":"What starts the action? · Part 2","spark_text":"An app has a button labelled Play. Pressing that button is an event. The app responds by playing a sound. The button itself is not the sound; it is the control that triggers the action. Without the event, this sound does not start.","why_it_matters":"An event connects something the user does with a response from the program.","question":"What is the event in this example, and what action does it trigger?"}},{"id":"grade8_t3_2026_w02_part1_spark","before":[{"title":"G8 T3 W02 Part 1 - Test your lunch map","spark_text":"We test the bridge with the same small weight each time. A bug makes the app show Rice after the user chooses Vegetables.","why_it_matters":"Test three lunch-app actions and explain one checked result.","question":"You choose Vegetables, but the app shows Rice. What did this test reveal?"},{"title":"G8 T3 W02 - Test and finish LunchApp - Words in use 1","spark_text":"Pressing button A provides an input to the program. The displayed number is an output from the counter. The variable count stores the number of button presses.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"A press changes the state · Part 1","spark_text":"A device starts with its light off. Each new button press changes the light to the opposite state: off becomes on, and on becomes off. This is a toggle. After two presses, the light is off again. The program must remember its current state between presses.","why_it_matters":"Remembering state lets the same input have the correct next effect.","question":"Starting with the light off, what state should it have after three presses? Explain."}},{"id":"grade8_t3_2026_w02_part2_spark","before":[{"title":"G8 T3 W02 Part 2 - Plan a club signup app","spark_text":"We test the app by choosing a lunch and checking the order message. The variable meal stores the chosen lunch.","why_it_matters":"Plan three app screens that keep and confirm a student’s club choice.","question":"How would you check that the meal variable kept the chosen lunch?"},{"title":"G8 T3 W02 - Test and finish LunchApp - Words in use 2","spark_text":"We test the program by pressing the button three times. The bug makes the app show the wrong lunch choice.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"A press changes the state · Part 2","spark_text":"A device starts with its light off. Each new button press changes the light to the opposite state: off becomes on, and on becomes off. This is a toggle. After two presses, the light is off again. The program must remember its current state between presses.","why_it_matters":"Remembering state lets the same input have the correct next effect.","question":"Starting with the light off, what state should it have after three presses? Explain."}},{"id":"grade8_t3_2026_w03_part1_spark","before":[{"title":"G8 T3 W03 Part 1 - Organise your app work","spark_text":"I save my spreadsheet as a file named ClubResults.xlsx. The folder keeps my three project files together.","why_it_matters":"Locate your saved app work and explain one checked part.","question":"Why is it easier to find three project files when they share one folder?"},{"title":"G8 T3 W03 - Explain and improve LunchApp - Words in use 1","spark_text":"The variable count stores the number of button presses. A button click is an event that starts the instructions. We test the program by pressing the button three times.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"What starts the action? · Part 1","spark_text":"An app has a button labelled Play. Pressing that button is an event. The app responds by playing a sound. The button itself is not the sound; it is the control that triggers the action. Without the event, this sound does not start.","why_it_matters":"An event connects something the user does with a response from the program.","question":"What is the event in this example, and what action does it trigger?"}},{"id":"grade8_t3_2026_w03_part2_spark","before":[{"title":"G8 T3 W03 Part 2 - Check and improve your app","spark_text":"We test the app by choosing a lunch and checking the order message. The feedback says the app should show the chosen lunch.","why_it_matters":"Use three test results to check your app’s confirmation message.","question":"A test shows the bridge bends. What feedback would help the builder improve it?"},{"title":"G8 T3 W03 - Explain and improve LunchApp - Words in use 2","spark_text":"The result of the test is a message showing the chosen lunch. The feedback says to check the displayed total after a reset.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"Explain what changed · Part 2","spark_text":"A student compares two versions of a poster. In the first, small text was hard to read. In the second, the text is larger, and three classmates can read it from the same distance. A reflection explains a change, its reason and the evidence of its effect.","why_it_matters":"Specific evidence makes a reflection more useful than simply saying 'It is better.'","question":"Which change did the student make, and what evidence suggests it helped?"}},{"id":"grade8_t3_2026_w04_part1_spark","before":[{"title":"G8 T3 W04 Part 1 - Plan your STEAM work","spark_text":"The paper bridge is a prototype we can test before building a stronger bridge. The small cardboard model shows the shape of the bridge.","why_it_matters":"Choose one project task and state how you will check it.","question":"What could you test with a paper prototype before building a stronger version?"},{"title":"G8 T3 W04 - Binary numbers - Words in use 1","spark_text":"One bit can store either 0 or 1. The binary number 101 represents five. The decimal number five is written as 101 in binary.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"Two symbols can represent information · Part 1","spark_text":"A display uses three lights. Each light is either off, written 0, or on, written 1. The patterns 001 and 010 use the same two symbols but put them in different positions. A binary representation uses two possible symbols or states to encode information.","why_it_matters":"Both the states and their positions can carry meaning.","question":"How are 001 and 010 different even though each has only one light on?"}},{"id":"grade8_t3_2026_w04_part2_spark","before":[{"title":"G8 T3 W04 Part 2 - Build your STEAM project","spark_text":"The paper bridge is a prototype we can test before building a stronger bridge. The small cardboard model shows the shape of the bridge.","why_it_matters":"Complete your planned task and record its result.","question":"What could you test with a paper prototype before building a stronger version?"},{"title":"G8 T3 W04 - Binary numbers - Words in use 2","spark_text":"Each digit in 101 is either zero or one. The leftmost place value in four-bit binary is eight.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"Two symbols can represent information · Part 2","spark_text":"A display uses three lights. Each light is either off, written 0, or on, written 1. The patterns 001 and 010 use the same two symbols but put them in different positions. A binary representation uses two possible symbols or states to encode information.","why_it_matters":"Both the states and their positions can carry meaning.","question":"How are 001 and 010 different even though each has only one light on?"}},{"id":"grade8_t3_2026_w07_part1_spark","before":[{"title":"G8 T3 W07 Part 1 - Calculate with Python","spark_text":"Typing 6 gives the Python program an input. The variable total stores the answer to the calculation.","why_it_matters":"Run a Python program that multiplies two whole numbers.","question":"Which variable could store a number typed by the user?"},{"title":"G8 T3 W07 - Python multiplication and debugging - Words in use 1","spark_text":"Pressing button A provides an input to the program. The variable count stores the number of button presses. The integer 4 can be stored in the quantity variable.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"A press changes the state · Part 1","spark_text":"A device starts with its light off. Each new button press changes the light to the opposite state: off becomes on, and on becomes off. This is a toggle. After two presses, the light is off again. The program must remember its current state between presses.","why_it_matters":"Remembering state lets the same input have the correct next effect.","question":"Starting with the light off, what state should it have after three presses? Explain."}},{"id":"grade8_t3_2026_w07_part2_spark","before":[{"title":"G8 T3 W07 Part 2 - Fix Python mistakes","spark_text":"A bug makes the total subtract instead of add. The variable total stores the answer to the calculation.","why_it_matters":"Correct five familiar mistakes and test the program.","question":"The total is wrong. Which stored values would you check first?"},{"title":"G8 T3 W07 - Python multiplication and debugging - Words in use 2","spark_text":"The displayed number is an output from the counter. The bug makes the app show the wrong lunch choice.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"Find the cause of the wrong result · Part 2","spark_text":"A game should add one point for each star, but it adds two. Eva checks the scoring instructions and finds two 'add 1' commands running for the same star. She removes one and tests again. Debugging means fi