er its current state between presses.","why_it_matters":"Remembering state lets the same input have the correct next effect.","question":"Starting with the light off, what state should it have after three presses? Explain."}},{"id":"grade9_t3_2026_w01_part1_spark","before":[{"title":"G9 T3 W01 Part 1 - Picture size in bits","spark_text":"Each pixel adds one tiny square of colour to the picture. A bit can store a 0 or a 1.","why_it_matters":"Calculate the data size of one digital picture in bits.","question":"If a picture has four pixels and each uses one bit, how many bits store its pixel data?"}],"after":{"title":"Two symbols can represent information · Part 1","spark_text":"A display uses three lights. Each light is either off, written 0, or on, written 1. The patterns 001 and 010 use the same two symbols but put them in different positions. A binary representation uses two possible symbols or states to encode information.","why_it_matters":"Both the states and their positions can carry meaning.","question":"How are 001 and 010 different even though each has only one light on?"}},{"id":"grade9_t3_2026_w01_part2_spark","before":[{"title":"G9 T3 W01 Part 2 - Picture and sound data","spark_text":"A sample stores one sound measurement from one moment. A sample rate of 8 means eight sound measurements each second in our small example.","why_it_matters":"Calculate picture and sound data sizes and explain one quality and size choice.","question":"At eight samples each second, how many sound measurements are taken in two seconds?"}],"after":{"title":"Make the message clear · Part 2","spark_text":"A recording contains a quiet voice and loud background music. Listeners struggle to understand the words. The editor lowers the music and tests the recording again. Sound quality includes how clearly the intended sound can be heard, not just how loud the whole recording is.","why_it_matters":"Listeners need to hear the message clearly.","question":"Why could lowering the music improve this recording more than making everything louder?"}},{"id":"grade9_t3_2026_w02_part1_spark","before":[{"title":"G9 T3 W02 Part 1 - Organise your work","spark_text":"Each pixel adds one tiny square of colour to the picture. A bit can store a 0 or a 1.","why_it_matters":"Find your picture and sound work and explain one checked answer.","question":"If a picture has four pixels and each uses one bit, how many bits store its pixel data?"},{"title":"G9 T3 W02 - Online dangers - Words in use 1","spark_text":"The phishing message asks for the school-account password. The unsafe download installs malware on the computer. The ransomware locks the project files and demands money.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"Pause before you send · Part 1","spark_text":"Leo receives a message that says, 'Send your password now or your account will close.' The message tries to make him act quickly. A password is private information. Instead of replying or using the message's link, Leo can open the service through its usual app and ask a trusted adult for help.","why_it_matters":"An urgent message can pressure someone into sharing private information.","question":"What should Leo do instead of sending his password? Explain why."}},{"id":"grade9_t3_2026_w02_part2_spark","before":[{"title":"G9 T3 W02 Part 2 - Picture and sound check","spark_text":"A sample stores one sound measurement from one moment. A sample rate of 8 means eight sound measurements each second in our small example.","why_it_matters":"Calculate two data sizes and explain two choices about quality and size.","question":"At eight samples each second, how many sound measurements are taken in two seconds?"},{"title":"G9 T3 W02 - Online dangers - Words in use 2","spark_text":"Keep your password private when a message asks you to share it. A separate backup provides protection against losing the only project copy.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"One copy is not enough · Part 2","spark_text":"Mia saves her project only on her laptop. The laptop stops working, and she cannot open the file. A backup is an extra copy kept somewhere else. If Mia had saved a backup in a separate location, she could use that copy to continue working.","why_it_matters":"A separate copy can protect work when the original is lost or damaged.","question":"How would a backup help Mia, and where could she keep it?"}},{"id":"grade9_t3_2026_w03_part1_spark","before":[{"title":"G9 T3 W03 Part 1 - Spot online dangers","spark_text":"A phishing message asks for a password on a fake login page. The malware damages files after an unsafe download.","why_it_matters":"Use a clue to identify one online danger and explain a safe response.","question":"Why should you check the source before entering a password or downloading a file?"},{"title":"G9 T3 W03 - Compare and explain online risks - Words in use 1","spark_text":"Saving only one copy creates a risk of losing the project. Repeated failed logins increase our concern about the probability of an account attack. A separate backup reduces the impact of losing the working file.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"One copy is not enough · Part 1","spark_text":"Mia saves her project only on her laptop. The laptop stops working, and she cannot open the file. A backup is an extra copy kept somewhere else. If Mia had saved a backup in a separate location, she could use that copy to continue working.","why_it_matters":"A separate copy can protect work when the original is lost or damaged.","question":"How would a backup help Mia, and where could she keep it?"}},{"id":"grade9_t3_2026_w03_part2_spark","before":[{"title":"G9 T3 W03 Part 2 - Choose a safe response","spark_text":"A phishing message asks for a password on a fake login page. The malware damages files after an unsafe download.","why_it_matters":"Explain a protection for ransomware and repeated password guessing.","question":"Why should you check the source before entering a password or downloading a file?"},{"title":"G9 T3 W03 - Compare and explain online risks - Words in use 2","spark_text":"The repeated password requests are evidence that the message is suspicious. A separate backup provides protection against losing the only project copy.","why_it_matters":"Use these words to explain what happens in this week’s Technology work.","question":"Choose one example. Explain what its vocabulary word means in that situation."}],"after":{"title":"One copy is not enough · Part 2","spark_text":"Mia saves her project only on her laptop. The laptop stops working, and she cannot open the file. A backup is an extra copy kept somewhere else. If Mia had saved a backup in a separate location, she could use that copy to continue working.","why_it_matters":"A separate copy can protect work when the original is lost or damaged.","question":"How would a backup help Mia, and where could she keep it?"}},{"id":"grade9_t3_2026_w04_part1_spark","before":[{"title":"G9 T3 W04 Part 1 - Plan your STEAM work","spark_text":"The paper bridge is a prototype we can test before building a stronger bridge. The small cardboard model shows the shape of the bridge.","why_it_matters":"Choose one project task and state how you will check it.","question":"What could you test with a paper prototype before building a stronger version?"},{"title":"G9 T3 W04 - Explain the message risk - Words in use 1","spark_text":"The phishing message asks for the school-account password. Saving only one copy creates a risk of losing the project. 